{"id": "172788", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=172788", "title": "Romansh phrasebook", "text": "Romansh (sometimes written as Romantsch or Romansch) is a language of the Romance family, spoken by between 36,000 and 70,000 people in the Swiss canton of Grisons. Though it is the least used of Switzerland's four official languages, it is still very much respected as a minority language and can be heard in schools and on television and radio.\nMost speakers speak one of a few different dialects, though in 1982 a standardized form was created."}
{"id": "172789", "revid": "144844", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=172789", "title": "Bandit Run", "text": "The Bandit Run is a re-enactment of the road travelled in the 1977 film \"Smokey and the Bandit\".\nUnderstand.\nThe 1977 film \"Smokey and the Bandit\" tells the story of running a semi-trailer full of Coors beer from Texarkana in Texas (the furthest east it could then be legally bought) to Atlanta, Georgia, with a 1977 Pontiac Trans Am as a blocking vehicle.\nThe film remains as a classic, and a contemporary depiction of the late golden age of trucking, American muscle cars, and citizen-band radio, as well as the rebellious spirit of the 1970s.\nThe \"Bandit Run\" is a reenactment of the journey portrayed in the 1977 film, \"Smokey and the Bandit\". The reenactment first took place on May 15, 2007 as a 30-year commemoration of the film which has become an annual event since. Participants, also known as \"Bandit Runners\", made this journey with the 1970s version of the Pontiac Trans Am which was the car the main character, Bo \"Bandit\" Darville (Burt Reynolds), drove in to make the journey.\nGo.\nThe film displays the westward journey from Atlanta to Texarkana in a short montage. Most of the plot takes place during the return trip. The 2007 \"Bandit Run\" went east from Texarkana to Atlanta. \n* \nThe main filming locations of the Smokey and the Bandit film trilogy.\nThe filming locations differ from the places mentioned in the \"Bandit Run\" itinerary and in the script of the film. For example, the scenes from Texarkana were instead filmed in the Atlanta exurb of Jonesboro, Georgia."}
{"id": "172791", "revid": "36420", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=172791", "title": "Sani Pass", "text": "The Sani Pass is a route into Lesotho from Underberg, South Africa, with breathtaking views. There are places to stay and activities at the top.\nUnderstand.\nThe pass is a winding road up to Lesotho. It was first crossed by vehicle in 1953.\nGet in.\nThe Sani Pass is a 4x4-only road. You can drive it yourself or several companies will take you up and down for 900 rand return (May 2019). Your hotel may be able to arrange transport. \nYou must take your passport, and birth certificates for minors.\nDo.\nSani Mountain Lodge can arrange horse riding and bike riding. Surprisingly horses are cheaper to rent than bicycles. There are several hikes nearby, including Lesotho's highest mountain Thabana Ntlenyana to the north and closer mountains to the south. \nThe hike to Thabana Ntlenyana is not difficult terrain, but the thin air (elevation 3482m) makes it harder. From Sani Mountain lodge it is an 8 hour minimum hike there and back, so make sure you set off early to be back before dark. You can download the route here[dead link] and open it on app E-Walk Free. Remember to download necessary maps while you still have internet access.\nThe hike to the south do not require maps. You can see the pyramid-shaped mountain from Sani Mountain Lodge; just head towards it. It takes 75 minutes to get there. There are three more peaks beyond that; you can choose how many you want to do. You can conquer them all and be back at the lodge within 6 hours.\nEat and drink.\nSani Mountain Lodge serves lunch, and also breakfast and dinner by arrangement. It supposedly contains Africa's highest pub, at 2874m, however Afriski has a bar at 3222m.\nSleep.\nMost people visit for a day-trip to have lunch, or stay one night, but it's worth spending a couple of days here (up to four nights to exhaust everything there is to do).\nGo next.\nYou can continue in to Lesotho, or return to Underberg. Underberg is very beautiful."}
{"id": "172809", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=172809", "title": "Larantuka", "text": "Larantuka is in Flores (Indonesia).\nTalk.\nLarantuka Malay (also known as Ende Malay), a local dialect over 80% cognate with Indonesian, is used as a lingua franca in this area. Portuguese is used in certain Catholic religious rituals.\nDo.\nEvents.\n\"Semana Santa\" (Holy Week), the week before Easter, an important time of religious celebration for the devoutly Catholic people of the Diocese of Larantuka."}
